# Task

Turn my references into a decisions file from which an agent and a developer can
derive principles, tokens and components. My descriptions use everyday language.
Your job is to translate them into observations and decisions, not judge them or
replace them with your own.

# Context

- Product and users: Players playing an online space trading game
- Brief or research results: .ai/specs/product-brief.md
- References: html mockups linked at the end of this prompt

# How to work

1. Read the brief and open every screenshot. If you cannot open a link, write
   “link, not opened” and rely only on my description. Without a screenshot, an
   observation is a paraphrase: mark what is unknown and ask for a screenshot.
2. Separate three things for each reference:
   - Observation: what is visible, described so two people would draw the same thing.
     Not “nice colors”, but e.g. “one saturated color covering the entire panel,
     ink-colored text, no gradient”.
   - Keep: one to three traits to bring into the product.
   - Do not keep: what we reject from this reference. Always fill this in, even
     if I left it empty, and mark it as a proposal.
3. Give each trait its own row and ID (e.g. R01.1, R01.2) and a family: color,
   typography, composition and density, components, motion and response, tone of
   voice. This gives each token precise provenance. Dislikes are decisions too:
   record what we do instead (e.g. “not warm enough” → warm grays), not just rejection.
4. Connect each reference to the product: why does this trait suit our users or
   the product’s purpose? If you cannot justify it, say so. A reference with no
   connection to the product is taste, not a decision.
5. From my words, propose 3 to 5 character words and as many anti-character words.
   Derive the anti-character from negative references.
6. For negative references, describe the signature: specific elements that make
   this appearance recognizable on screen and in code. Always add a “generic AI
   appearance” negative reference, unless one of mine already covers it; then merge them.
7. Look in the brief and research for negative references based on user knowledge,
   not taste (e.g. a user says “I do not want a course, I want an answer”, so we
   reject progress bars and certificates). Mark these as proposals.
8. Check consistency: where references contradict one another (e.g. two palettes,
   dense versus spacious), list the conflict and propose a resolution. Do not decide alone.
9. Do not invent references or values I did not supply. If something is missing
   (e.g. no reference says anything about typography), ask; mark suggestions as proposals.

# Questions before saving

Before saving the file, ask me questions, at most five at once. List each proposal
you added separately so I can accept or reject them individually, not as a bundle.
Ask about:
- descriptions too general to verify on screen,
- contradictions between references,
- families with no reference,
- every proposal you added yourself.

